<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Chouchi</h1><p class="st-meta">296 CE – 506 CE (approx.) · capital Chouchi, Wudu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The mountain stronghold of the Di people's Yang clan in southern Gansu, which survived through the Sixteen Kingdoms by balancing northern and southern dynasties.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 296 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-maosou">Yang Maosou</a>, chief of a Di clan from the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-river">Han River</a> region, led his people to Chouchi, a flat-topped mountain near modern Xihe in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, whose natural defences made it a refuge during the collapse of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Yang rulers took titles from whichever dynasty was strongest, Jin, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> or the southern courts, while governing their Di subjects by their own customs. Their realm in the hills between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> controlled routes between north and south. It was destroyed by Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 371 and revived in 385, later as several related principalities centred on Wudu.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under pressure from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> and the southern dynasties, the Yang principalities were absorbed one by one; the register follows the end of their independence in 506, when Northern Wei took Wuxing. Yang descendants kept local power in the region into the 6th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chouchi.jpg" alt="Chouchi" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_317_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>296 CE – 506 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chouchi, Wudu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yang Maosou, Yang Nandang</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Di</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qiuchi, Wudu Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=401&amp;focus=chouchi" aria-label="Chouchi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7386 953 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chouchi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7706 1209L7769 1191M7706 1209L7771 1192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7706 1209h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7706 1209h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=401&amp;focus=chouchi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

